    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that both the two-digit number \\( ab \\) (where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the outcomes of two fair 6-sided dice) and the digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are all divisible by 3, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the possible outcomes for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n2. Determine which numbers from 1 to 6 are divisible by 3.\n3. Count the number of favorable outcomes.\n4. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes.\n5. Find the probability.\n\nFirst, let's identify the possible outcomes for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). Both \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) can be any number from 1 to 6, so there are \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\) possible outcomes when two dice are rolled.\n\nNext, we list the numbers from 1 to 6 and identify which of these are divisible by 3:\n\\[ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \\]\nThe numbers divisible by 3 are:\n\\[ 3, 6 \\]\n\nSo, there are 2 favorable outcomes for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) being divisible by 3 each (i.e., \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\), or \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\), or \\( a = 6 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\), or \\( a = 6 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\)).\n\nNow, we count the number of favorable outcomes for the two-digit number \\( ab \\) being divisible by 3. The number \\( ab \\) can be written as \\( 10a + b \\). For \\( ab \\) to be divisible by 3, \\( 10a + b \\) must be divisible by 3. Let's check each possible value of \\( a \\):\n- If \\( a = 3 \\), then \\( 10 \\cdot 3 + b = 30 + b \\). \\( 30 + b \\) is divisible by 3 for \\( b = 3 \\) (since \\( 30 + 3 = 33 \\)).\n- If \\( a = 6 \\), then \\( 10 \\cdot 6 + b = 60 + b \\). \\( 60 + b \\) is divisible by 3 for \\( b = 6 \\) (since \\( 60 + 6 = 66 \\)).\n\nSo, the favorable outcomes for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are \\( (3, 3) \\) and \\( (6, 6) \\). There are 2 favorable outcomes.\n\nThe probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[ \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{2}{36} = \\frac{1}{18} \\]\n\nThus, the probability that both the two-digit number \\( ab \\) and the digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are all divisible by 3 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{18}} \\]",
